  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "exception-hierarchy-design",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Designing domain exception hierarchies with a base DomainError class and specific subclasses. | Exception Hierarchy Design",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "hierarchy.extensibility",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Define additional DomainError subclasses (such as UserNotFoundError or InvalidOrderStateError) to express more precise error conditions without leaking transport concerns.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "domain.explicit-raises",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Document all domain exceptions a method may raise in a Raises section of the docstring so callers know which failures to handle.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "domain.no-http-coupling",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Domain exceptions must not depend on any HTTP framework; they should be pure Python exceptions that can be used in CLI tools, background jobs, or tests.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "domain.exception-propagation",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Allow domain exceptions to propagate up through the call stack to the transport layer, which is responsible for translating them to appropriate responses.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "domain.specific-over-generic",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Prefer specific exception types over generic ones; raise UserNotFoundError rather than ResourceNotFoundError when the context is clear and the distinction matters.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "exception-context-messages",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Guidelines for exception message formatting and including context in exceptions. | Exception Context and Messages",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "context.informative-messages",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Exception messages should be informative for debugging, including relevant identifiers, operation names, and state that led to the error.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "context.constructor-params",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Accept context as constructor parameters (resource_id, field_name, expected_value) and build the message from them rather than requiring callers to format messages.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "context.attribute-access",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Store context as exception attributes so handlers can access structured data (exc.resource_id, exc.field) in addition to the formatted message.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "context.no-sensitive-data",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Never include passwords, tokens, or other secrets in exception messages; these may be logged or exposed in error responses.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "context.localization-ready",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Keep exception messages technical and developer-facing; user-facing error messages should be generated by the transport layer using exception attributes.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "error-handling-patterns",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Patterns for catching, wrapping, and re-raising exceptions in Python code. | Error Handling Patterns",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "handling.wrap-low-level",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Catch low-level exceptions (database errors, network errors, I/O errors) and wrap them in domain-appropriate exceptions to hide implementation details.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "handling.exception-chaining",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Use raise DomainException() from original_exception to preserve the exception chain for debugging while presenting a domain-appropriate exception to callers.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "handling.specific-catches",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Catch specific exception types rather than broad except Exception blocks; only catch exceptions you can handle meaningfully.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "handling.try-finally-cleanup",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Use try/finally blocks to ensure cleanup of resources (file handles, connections) regardless of whether exceptions occur.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "handling.reraise-unknown",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "If you catch an exception and cannot handle it appropriately, re-raise it rather than swallowing it or returning a generic error.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "exception-testing-patterns",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Testing strategies for exceptions using pytest.raises and assertion patterns. | Exception Testing Patterns",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "testing.pytest-raises",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Use pytest.raises(ExceptionType) as a context manager to assert that code raises expected exceptions.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "testing.exception-attributes",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Assert on exception attributes (exc_info.value.resource_id, exc_info.value.field) to verify exceptions carry correct context.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "testing.message-content",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Assert that exception messages contain expected content using in operator or string matching when message format is part of the contract.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "testing.propagation",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Write tests that verify exceptions propagate correctly through the call stack without being inadvertently caught or transformed.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "testing.edge-cases",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Include tests for edge cases such as empty inputs, boundary values, and concurrent operations that may raise exceptions.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "anti.sentinel-values",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Returning sentinel values (None, -1, empty string) instead of raising exceptions to indicate error conditions.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "anti.string-exceptions",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Using strings or generic Exception with string messages instead of typed exception classes, losing type safety and structured error handling.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "anti.exposed-internals",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Including stack traces, SQL statements, internal paths, or other implementation details in exception messages that may be exposed to users.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/python/python.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "anti.exception-control-flow",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Using exceptions for normal control flow rather than exceptional conditions, which harms performance and code readability.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/fastapi/fastapi.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "http-exception-usage",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "When to use fastapi.HTTPException for HTTP-layer concerns versus domain exceptions. | HTTP Exception Usage",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/fastapi/fastapi.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "http.reserve-for-transport",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Reserve fastapi.HTTPException for HTTP-layer concerns such as malformed requests, authentication problems, and rate-limiting decisions.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/fastapi/fastapi.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "http.domain-exceptions-first",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Services and domain components should raise DomainError subclasses; the transport layer translates these to HTTP responses via global handlers.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/fastapi/fastapi.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "http.not-in-services",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Do not raise HTTPException from services; this couples domain logic to HTTP concerns and makes services harder to reuse in non-HTTP contexts.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/fastapi/fastapi.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "http.appropriate-cases",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Appropriate uses of HTTPException include authentication failures at the router level, middleware rejections, and explicit rate limit enforcement.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/fastapi/fastapi.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "http.prefer-global-handlers",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Prefer global exception handlers that translate domain exceptions to HTTP responses over scattering HTTPException raises throughout the codebase.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/fastapi/fastapi.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "domain-to-http-mapping",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "How global handlers map domain exceptions to HTTP responses with appropriate status codes. | Domain to HTTP Mapping",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/fastapi/fastapi.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "mapping.pattern",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Global exception handlers translate domain exceptions to HTTP responses, mapping exception types to appropriate status codes and ErrorCode values.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/fastapi/fastapi.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "mapping.common-mappings",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Map ResourceNotFoundError to 404 Not Found with a resource-specific ErrorCode, DomainValidationError to 400 with VALIDATION_ERROR and details, and UnauthorizedError to 401 or 403 depending on semantics.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/fastapi/fastapi.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "mapping.centralized",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Keep domain-to-HTTP mapping logic centralized in global handlers registered in create_app rather than scattering it across routers.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/fastapi/fastapi.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "mapping.router-behavior",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "Allow DomainError subclasses to propagate out of routers so the global DomainError handler can convert them to AppError responses; do not catch and re-wrap them as HTTPException.",
    "splits": []
  },
  {
    "source_file": "docs/development/fastapi/fastapi.exception-patterns.yml",
    "id": "mapping.extensibility",
    "origin_type": "split_only",
    "source_text": "When adding new DomainError subclasses, update the domain handler to map them to appropriate HTTP responses with suitable status codes and error codes.",
    "splits": []
  }
